+### pi-durable source contract
+
+The durable coordinator is long-lived by design and is where GLG's own wording
+will mostly accumulate, so the quality bar for this source is **user-utterance
+fidelity, time-axis accuracy, and incremental cost** — in that order. Measured
+basis: the 2026-10-06 API 0 probe against a synthetic faux-provider DB built
+from the pinned durable source (`cd32f772`); stamp its receipt in ROADMAP
+History when the source lands.
+
+- **Discovery.** Path grammar above, anchored in full (24-hex bucket,
+  `\d{13}-<UUIDv4>`, `session.sqlite`). Sibling `<dir>.lock` directories and
+  `-wal` / `-shm` files are never admitted or copied. `durable_schema.version`
+  above the supported version is **refused by name**, never guessed. A durable
+  root that exists but admits zero sessions is a doctor WARN — the 2026-08-07
+  silent-darkness lesson applies to a second path grammar too.
+- **Snapshot, not copy.** A live durable DB keeps its committed rows in the WAL;
+  copying `session.sqlite` alone yields a file with **no tables at all**
+  (measured). The corpus copy is made with `VACUUM INTO` from a read-only
+  connection **on the host that owns the DB**, producing a single self-contained
+  file (journal mode `delete`, no WAL header). `immutable=1` and separate DB/WAL
+  rsync are forbidden. The native writer, its lock and `Harness.open` are never
+  touched. A snapshot holds one short read transaction; a held reader can delay
+  the native `close()` checkpoint by up to its 5 s busy timeout (measured), so
+  the snapshot must never hold a long read.
+- **Append-only, at the row level.** Durable `entries` and `conversations` are
+  INSERT-only, so a later snapshot is a superset. The corpus path is replaced by
+  a newer snapshot only when the new file passes `quick_check`, has a supported
+  schema, and contains every entry id the old one held; otherwise the old copy
+  stays and the gather reports it. An unchanged source (`durable_metadata.next_seq`
+  equal) produces no replacement, so mtime and manifest stay still.
+- **Chunks from physical rows only.** Fork inheritance is resolved by scan,
+  not storage — reading rows directly yields each entry exactly once; reading
+  per-conversation history would duplicate inherited rows.
+- **Classification by entry kind and placing submission, not by role.**
+  `pi.compaction` and handoff `pi.reset` carry `role: "user"`; a role filter would
+  index summaries as GLG's words. `pi.user` is GLG only when its placing
+  submission has no `request_id` (TUI input). `entwurf-doorbell:*` is a harness
+  notice (dropped, receiver unchanged); `subagent:*` is an agent brief (dropped,
+  receiver → agent); an unknown `request_id` prefix is dropped and counted
+  (fail closed). Assistant text inherits the conversation's receiver (the RAIL 5
+  rule shared with `pi` / `claude`); `stopReason` `error` / `aborted` partials are
+  dropped. `pi.tool-result` and `pi.system` are never embedded — the sibling
+  message body lands in a tool result, which is why agent↔agent traffic stays out.
+- **Identity.** chunk id `<corpus path>:e<entryId>[#part]`; `lineNumber` carries
+  the entry id. `entries.id` is per-DB monotonic and stable, so a chunk never
+  changes id between snapshots.
+- **Time.** `model[0].timestamp` (wall-clock ms) is the stored timestamp.
+  `commit_seq` orders; `documents.created_at` is a commit sequence, never a time.
+- **Watermark.** per corpus file, the max entry id already embedded. Indexing
+  appends chunks above it and never deletes the file's rows first — a durable
+  DB is never rewritten, so the JSONL stale path (delete-by-file, re-embed whole
+  file) would re-embed a months-long coordinator on every sync.
+- **Provenance.** device from the corpus path segment; project from the root
+  conversation's `pi.agent.cwd`, normalized exactly like `extractProjectName`,
+  cross-checked against the bucket hash. The meta-record is not consulted.
 
